Recursive Executable-Core Contract
===============================================================================

Status: S2 recovery, S3 typed views, and the S5 certification implementation
complete; S4 semantic completion open; the S6 registry candidate is staged,
pending S4 qualification.

Current qualified canonical progress is 328 / 539. The component implements
clean, lossless recognition, local malformed-input recovery, typed access,
source semantics, and canonical certification for all 80 rules. This unmerged
branch stages those 80 productions as one Phase 2I registry candidate, bringing
the target inventory to 408 / 539; it does not activate them while S4 remains
incomplete. The generated implementation boundary remains one strongly
connected component.
`docs/design/syntax-v0.4-continuation-plan.mec` is the current delivery plan
after the R1-R6 integration rebase.

The authoritative member inventory is
`docs/design/grammar-audit/phase-2i-recursive-core.tsv`. The generated count is
authoritative; no manual 33-rule, 83-rule, family-total, or handwritten member
assumption defines this phase.

The authoritative representation inventory is
`docs/design/grammar-audit/phase-2i-syntax-schema.tsv`. It freezes module
ownership and concrete tree emission for the same 80 rules: 71 unconditional
nodes, seven conditional precedence nodes, and two transparent aliases. It
reserves 73 new append-only syntax kinds and reuses five existing kinds.

1. Grammar and audit authority
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The exact `ebnf:canonical` fence in `docs/design/specification.mec` is the
normative grammar graph. The deterministic
`docs/design/grammar-audit/canonical-dependencies.tsv` report is generated from
that grammar and records its direct child and reverse parent edges.

`docs/design/grammar-audit/ports.tsv` records the canonical rule inventory and
rule-level syntax, source-semantic, activation, node-policy, phase, and audit
disposition evidence. It is audit metadata and the deterministic RuleId
registry input, not parser or runtime dispatch. Its `certified` semantic status is distinct from the
milestone-level behavior-demonstration gate. Its separate `activation-status`
keeps all 80 Phase 2I rows at `candidate` until the completion record reports
every required behavior as demonstrated. The recursive-core component is the
exact Phase 2I candidate set:
it starts at `expression`, follows the generated canonical dependencies, and
contains one complete strongly connected component.

`phase-2i-recursive-core.tsv` defines membership and dependencies.
`phase-2i-syntax-schema.tsv` defines module ownership and concrete tree
emission. The schema does not replace or duplicate the dependency graph.

The dependency extractor does not inspect retiring Rust parser calls. An
independent repository test normalizes implementation-level descriptive
primitives and the grammar meta-parser's whitespace-only terminal in memory,
parses the complete grammar with the canonical grammar root, and requires the
typed grammar syntax to reproduce every generated edge.

2. Canonical authority only
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

All Phase 2I production code uses the canonical event-based parser and the
canonical specification. The Phase 2I-C recognizer does not call, wrap,
translate, or extend a Nom parser.

`ParseString` and `ParseResult` do not appear in canonical recursive-core code.
Nom parser functions are not test or production dependencies of canonical
parser, typed-syntax, validation, certification, or source-semantic modules.

The dependency extractor does not inspect old Rust parser calls. The canonical
grammar graph is the only dependency authority.

3. Atomic activation candidate
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The S6 candidate assigns every generated member to Phase 2I only after the
complete rule-level closure:

```text
parses
recovers
exposes typed syntax
implements its specified semantic behavior
passes canonical conformance
passes ambiguity tests
passes resource-limit tests
```

S6 of `docs/design/syntax-v0.4-continuation-plan.mec` stages the complete
reviewed closure atomically. No partial candidate registry state exists. The
transition remains inactive until the separate S4 behavior-completion gate and
S6 integration qualification pass. A direct registry-to-completion test rejects
an `active` Phase 2I row while any required behavior remains incomplete.

4. Module design
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Phase 2I-B freezes this exact ownership division:

```text
kinds             11
literals           1
variables          2
subscripts         6
structures        23
calls               4
patterns            7
comprehensions      4
fsm                 7
precedence         13
expressions          2
                   --
total               80
```

The planned parser layout is:

```text
parser/canonical/recursive_core/
  mod.rs
  kinds.rs
  literals.rs
  variables.rs
  subscripts.rs
  structures.rs
  calls.rs
  patterns.rs
  comprehensions.rs
  fsm.rs
  precedence.rs
  expressions.rs
```

The typed syntax layout mirrors it under:

```text
ast/recursive_core/
```

Executable typed forms enter through the engine-owned source frontend defined
by `docs/design/syntax-v0.4-continuation-plan.mec`. No recursive-core
parallel syntax-to-value tree is added to the syntax crate.

The implementation uses ordinary mutually recursive Rust functions. It does
not introduce a parser generator, boxed parser closures, a Nom-like combinator
framework, or one monolithic `recursive_core.rs` module.

Phase 2I-C created the parser directory above and implemented the complete clean
recognizer. S2 added bounded local recovery, S3 added the typed syntax surface,
S4 owns engine source-semantic completion, and S5 closed the certification
implementation. S6 stages all 80 recursive rules as one atomic registry
candidate; qualification and activation wait for the S4 behavior gate.
Canonical document-root completion remains the S7 milestone; product cutover
and removal are reserved for S8.

`MATCH-001` records the canonical expression-match whitespace rule. The
optional suffix owns that whitespace only when the following question mark
matches.

5. Complete-form selection
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Shared initial punctuation does not choose a construct. Candidates remain
transactional and nondiagnostic. A construct owns recovery only after its
complete distinctive signature has won.

The required distinctions include:

```text
(1)               parenthetical expression
(1, 2)            tuple

[1 2]             matrix
[x | x <- xs]     matrix comprehension

{a: b}            record
{1: b}            map
{a: b, 1: c}      map after the complete record candidate fails cleanly
{a, b}            set
{a | a <- xs}     set comprehension
{}                 empty set
{:}                empty map

foo                variable
foo(x)             function call
foo[1]             slice

:some              atom
:some(value)       tuple structure
```

6. Precedence syntax
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

`l1` through `l7` remain canonical rules. A level with no operator adds no
unconditional wrapper node. A level with one or more operators creates one
chain node. The Phase 2D operator nodes remain authoritative.

The planned chain nodes are:

```text
LogicExpression
ComparisonExpression
AdditiveExpression
MultiplicativeExpression
PowerExpression
TableExpression
SetExpression
```

`formula` and `pattern-array-item` are transparent aliases. The five reused
kinds are `Expression`, `KindAnnotation`, `AdditiveExpression`,
`ParentheticalExpression`, and `VariableDefine`; every other node-valued
schema row uses one of the 73 new append-only kinds reserved by Phase 2I-B.

7. Recovery ownership
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A recognized operator owns a missing right-hand side. An owned delimiter form
owns a missing closer. Speculative alternatives emit no diagnostics.

Unexpected physical source remains under `ERROR`. Absent required syntax
becomes `MISSING`. Every recovery loop must consume physical source, insert
once, return, or exhaust a hard resource limit.

The S2 implementation distinguishes absence from unexpected source before it
chooses a recovery action. Delimiter scans balance nested parentheses,
brackets, and braces, ignore delimiters inside quoted source, stop before the
owner or ancestor closer, and charge a cumulative recovery-byte budget.
Diagnostic codes, canonical rule IDs, anchors, and recovery actions are stable
across repeated parses. All 80 direct rules retain their configured partial
node, or the configured child for a transparent rule, on committed failure.

S2 qualification follow-up (2026-09-12): the nine review findings and the
variable-range dispatch regression each have a named test in
`src/syntax/tests/canonical_phase_2i_review_regressions.rs`. Review IDs are
recorded beside their tests. Evidence includes direct/shared paths, selected
owners, later children, physical delimiters, missing/error structure, diagnostic
fields and anchors, and exact source reconstruction. The resource suite adds
13,203 parses across 26 fixtures: every fuel/event budget from 16 through 256,
plus nesting, diagnostic-count, and recovery-byte limits. Both formerly unfinished resource tests hang on historical parent
`0f846c680` (each stopped after five seconds); both now finish. Numeric and
recursive parents retain resource failures, and finalized error envelopes
cannot lose source through later lookahead. Descendants still require restacking
and qualification at their resulting heads.

8. Matrix presentation
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

There is one semantic `Matrix` and one lossless `Matrix` syntax node. Delimiter
and layout presentation remain in concrete tokens. There is no `InlineMatrix`
semantic variant, no `Matrix2d` semantic variant, no detached presentation
metadata, and no formatter heuristic.

Opening and closing delimiter styles are independently preserved. Any later
normalization is an explicit syntax transformation rather than inferred
semantic reconstruction.

9. Root boundary
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Phase 2I itself added no public parser root. S7 extends the current canonical
root surface to:

```text
Canonical + Grammar
Canonical + Document
```

This is the complete current parser-root API.

10. Historical delivery sequence
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

```text
2I-A  generated boundary and architecture
2I-B  syntax schema and minimal kernel support
2I-C  complete recursive parser
2I-D  recovery, typed views, and source semantics
2I-E  canonical certification and atomic registry activation
```

This sequence records the plan used to reach Phase 2I-C. The combined 2I-D and
2I-E steps are superseded by the independently gated S1-S6 milestones in
`docs/design/syntax-v0.4-continuation-plan.mec`. The current plan uses canonical
typed syntax, engine-owned source semantics, and specification-derived
certification.

Clean recognition, local lossless recovery, and typed views cover all 80
mutually recursive productions against the frozen schema. The S5 canonical
certification implementation is complete: comprehension, pattern, and typed FSM witnesses
produce validated revision-six artifacts, and all thirteen source-completion
behavior tests pass. Resident comprehensions borrow tuple, array, and tagged-pattern
components for primitive bindings, including repeated-binding checks. Compound
match results and nested match bodies and guards execute through typed control and
ordinary construction providers. S4 still owns composite comprehension bindings
and yields, computed pattern blocks, composition with nested comprehensions, and
the FSM resident continuation owner. S6 stages the atomic 80-row activation
candidate here, but it remains inactive and unqualified until S4 completes. The
machine-checked `grammar-audit/phase-2i-semantic-completion.tsv` record
distinguishes artifact readiness from behavior demonstrated on a named target.
The FSM artifact row is accepted, but its resident-runtime row is intentionally
unavailable and required for S6, so artifact evidence cannot open the gate.

Shared parenthesis, bracket, brace, colon, identifier, and kind prefixes are
handled without exponential reparsing. Expression-level set and matrix
comprehensions retain their formal priority over formula parsing; set
comprehensions are not factor forms. Complete record candidates may fall
through to maps, and that selection remains measured-linear for nested
record-looking maps. Matrix nodes retain leading whitespace, physical newlines,
and box borders. Every recursive repetition propagates a committed child
without discarding its diagnostic or parent structure. The component remains
independent of the canonical document root added by S7.
